  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\n- Mostly sunny today, cool tonight\n\nHigh pressure stretching from western Quebec southwest across the\nwestern Lakes will bring another day of tranquil weather with\nseasonal highs in the upper 70s, followed by lows in the lower to\nmid 50s tonight. The ridge will then begin to drift eastward\ntonight.\n\n- Chance of showers/storms Tuesday night/Wednesday, weekend\n\nSouth winds will increase Tuesday as the surface high moves east\nand the upper ridge moves overhead. What we'll see is an increase\nin heat and humidity as temperatures climb into the lower to mid\n80s dewpoints rise into the 60s. The upper ridge will be flattened\nby a short wave moving across the northern tier of states behind a\nsurface cold front. The LLJ increases to 40 kts Tuesday night \nahead of the cold front. As precipitable water values increase to\n2 inches, we may see some heavy rain develop as convection\ndevelops along/ahead of the front. Latest RRFS progs show\nconvection developing over the northern half of the cwa after 06z\nWednesday. This is also where the nose of the LLJ is and highest\nMUCAPE. It's worth noting the GFS is not bullish on CAPE Tuesday\nnight and the RRFS has backed off a bit too. That said, we'll need\nto watch for localized flooding as the upper flow becomes more\nparallel to the frontal boundary, which could lead to training\nechos. \n\nGiven the high PWATs, upper short wave, and approaching cold\nfront, PoPs in the 90 percent range seem reasonable late Tuesday\nnight and Wednesday. Once the front moves south Wednesday\nafternoon, the precipitation will go with it, leading to a couple\nof dry days late in the week. Highs will fall back into the 70s\nand dewpoints will retreat to the 50s. \n\nThe next cold front will approach Saturday. At this point, models\nshow the best upper dynamics well north into Canada which may\nresult in lower PoPs...20-30 percent...late Saturday into Sunday.",
